lio_ha -- TrueNAS LIO high-availability forwarding module
==========================================================

This module implements active/standby HA for the LIO SCSI target
subsystem on TrueNAS.  It is loaded on both the ACTIVE node (which
owns the storage pool) and the STANDBY node (which re-exports the
same LUNs to initiators over iSCSI and FC).

The module has two main components:

  ha_fwd (lio_ha_fwd.c)
    STANDBY side.  Implements the lio_ha_ops callbacks that LIO
    core calls when lio_ha_forward_active == 1.  Serialises se_cmd
    objects to the wire format, sends them to ACTIVE, receives
    responses, and completes the original se_cmd.

  ha_recv (lio_ha_recv.c)
    ACTIVE side.  A private LIO fabric driver with no configfs
    presence.  Accepts SESSION_CONNECT messages and creates synthetic
    se_sessions; dispatches forwarded commands to LIO core for local
    execution; returns results to STANDBY.

Module state is held in the global struct lio_ha_cfg (lio_ha.h) and
exposed through configfs attributes at /sys/kernel/config/lio_ha/.

The LIO core is modified in target_core_transport.c, target_core_pr.c,
target_core_tpg.c, and target_core_device.c under #ifdef CONFIG_TRUENAS.
The core-side architecture overview is in target_core_ha.h.


REQUIREMENTS
============

The following constraints apply when ALUA HA mode is in use (i.e. when
lio_ha_forward_active is set on STANDBY).

Static initiator ACLs required
  Every initiator that connects to STANDBY must have a named ACL entry
  (specific IQN or WWPN) configured on both nodes.  When an initiator
  connects to STANDBY, ha_recv on ACTIVE looks up the real node_acl by
  initiator name in the real target TPG.  If no matching ACL is found,
  session creation is refused and all forwarded commands for that session
  receive SAM_STAT_BUSY.

  Dynamic ACL mode (generate_node_acls=1, "demo mode") is therefore
  incompatible with ALUA HA: ACTIVE will not have nacls for initiators
  that have only connected to STANDBY, so SESSION_CONNECT is refused.

Identical LIO configuration on both nodes
  LUN mappings, initiator ACLs, CHAP credentials, and portal groups must
  be configured identically on both nodes.  ha_recv on ACTIVE uses the
  real target TPG's LUN mappings and ACLs to route forwarded commands;
  any divergence between ACTIVE and STANDBY configurations will cause
  LUN lookup failures or incorrect PR I_T nexus attribution.


ROLES
=====

ACTIVE
  Owns the storage pool; runs full LIO iblock/fileio backends.
  The ha_recv fabric driver accepts the TCP connection from STANDBY
  and creates a synthetic se_session for each initiator session
  that STANDBY reports via SESSION_CONNECT.  Forwarded commands
  execute against the real backends under the correct I_T nexus
  and PR context, and results are returned to STANDBY.

STANDBY
  Re-exports the same LUNs.  iblock/fileio backends are configured
  but not opened until failover (lio_ha_forward_active blocks local
  execution).  Every incoming SCSI command, TMR, and PR OUT is
  serialised to the wire and sent to ACTIVE for execution.  LUN
  mappings, CHAP credentials, and initiator ACLs are configured
  identically on both nodes.


TCP CONNECTION
==============

Both nodes run a listen socket and a connect loop simultaneously,
each bound to local_addr:port and connecting to peer_addr:port.
The first TCP connection established -- whether inbound or outbound
-- becomes the active link.  This symmetric model avoids any need
to designate one node as "server" and handles simultaneous restarts
gracefully: whichever node's TCP SYN succeeds first wins.

On link loss, both sides start reconnecting immediately.  The
connect side uses exponential backoff (1 s to 30 s between tries).

Default port: 999.

Threads:

  ha_listen_task   binds, listens, loops on kernel_accept.  On
                   accept calls ha_tcp_on_connected().

  ha_connect_task  loops: if disconnected, tries kernel_connect with
                   a 5 s timeout; exponential backoff on failure.
                   On success calls ha_tcp_on_connected().

  ha_rx_task       started per-connection inside ha_tcp_on_connected().
                   Reads wire header + payload in a loop, dispatches
                   by channel and message type.  On socket error
                   calls ha_tcp_on_disconnected() and exits.

Locking:

  ha_tcp_lock (mutex)   protects the active socket pointer, ha_rx_task
                        reference, and ha_state in lio_ha_cfg.

  ha_send_lock (mutex)  serialises callers of lio_ha_tcp_send() so
                        that header and payload are written atomically.
                        ha_tcp_on_disconnected() acquires this lock
                        before sock_release() to prevent freeing the
                        socket while a send is in progress.


WIRE PROTOCOL
=============

All integer fields are big-endian (__be*).  Full struct definitions
are in lio_ha_wire.h, which has no kernel dependencies and can be
read in isolation.

Every message is framed as:

  lio_ha_wire_hdr (8 bytes)
    __be32 channel   0 = CTL, 1 = DATA
    __be32 length    payload bytes, NOT including this header

  [lio_ha_data_hdr (8 bytes) -- DATA channel only, immediately after
   the wire header]
    __be64 cmd_cookie   ties this DATA message to a CMD_FORWARD or
                        CMD_RESPONSE

  payload

CTL-channel payloads begin with lio_ha_msg_hdr (8 bytes):

    __be32 type      enum lio_ha_msg_type
    __be32 reserved  must be zero


CHANNELS
========

Channel 0 (CTL)
  Carries all control messages: session lifecycle, I/O forwarding,
  TMR forwarding, PR replication, and LUN sync.  Each payload starts
  with lio_ha_msg_hdr so the receiver dispatches by type.

Channel 1 (DATA)
  Carries raw SCSI data for forwarded commands.  Each DATA payload
  is preceded by an 8-byte lio_ha_data_hdr containing a cmd_cookie
  that ties the data to its CMD_FORWARD (WRITE, S->A) or CMD_RESPONSE
  (READ, A->S).  No lio_ha_msg_hdr on this channel.


MESSAGE TYPES
=============

SESSION_CONNECT (S->A)
  Sent when an initiator connects to a STANDBY target.  Carries
  the initiator IQN/WWPN, target IQN/WWPN, fabric name, and TPG tag.
  ACTIVE calls target_ha_lookup_tpg() to find the matching TPG,
  looks up the node_acl for initiator_name, and creates a synthetic
  se_session keyed by STANDBY's se_session pointer (session_id).
  The synthetic session points to the real node_acl so that LUN
  mappings and PR per-I_T nexus tracking are correct.  If no matching
  TPG or ACL is found, session creation is refused; subsequent
  CMD_FORWARD messages for that session_id are answered with
  SAM_STAT_BUSY so STANDBY retries.

  TPG tag translation: each target has a real TPG and a portal-less
  phantom TPG on each node.  Node A real tags are < LIO_HA_NODE_B_TPG_OFFSET
  (32000); Node B real tags are >= it.  STANDBY translates its local
  real TPG tag to the peer (ACTIVE) tag before sending SESSION_CONNECT:
  if local_tag >= 32000, peer_tag = local_tag - 32000; otherwise
  peer_tag = local_tag + 32000.  This ensures ACTIVE's lookup finds
  the real TPG (which holds the initiator's node_acl) rather than the
  phantom TPG (which has no real ACLs and no portals).

  SESSION_CONNECT replay on link recovery: STANDBY maintains a
  shadow session list (ha_main_sess_list, protected by
  ha_main_sess_lock) that tracks every live initiator session.
  An entry is added in ha_main_session_create() before the TCP send,
  and removed in ha_main_session_destroy().  This handles the window
  where an initiator logs in while the TCP link is down: the send
  fails, but when the link recovers ha_main_on_connect() snapshots
  the list and replays SESSION_CONNECT for every tracked session.
  ACTIVE's lio_ha_recv_session_create() is idempotent -- a duplicate
  session_id is silently ignored -- so replaying a SESSION_CONNECT
  that already succeeded is harmless.  The shadow list is flushed
  when forward_active transitions 1->0 (failover), since STANDBY is
  becoming ACTIVE and replay is no longer needed.

SESSION_DISCONNECT (S->A)
  Sent when the initiator session tears down on STANDBY.  ACTIVE
  removes the synthetic se_session identified by session_id.

CMD_FORWARD (S->A)
  One per SCSI command.  Carries session_id, cmd_cookie (STANDBY's
  se_cmd pointer, echoed back in CMD_RESPONSE), LUN, data direction,
  expected transfer length, and the full CDB (up to 32 bytes).

  For WRITE commands: write data is sent on the DATA channel BEFORE
  CMD_FORWARD.  ACTIVE buffers the data keyed by cmd_cookie; it is
  ready when CMD_FORWARD arrives and triggers backend submission.

  For READ and NONE: no DATA message precedes CMD_FORWARD.

CMD_RESPONSE (A->S)
  Returns SCSI status (one byte) and optional sense data.  For READ
  commands: read data is sent on the DATA channel BEFORE CMD_RESPONSE.
  STANDBY's rx loop buffers the DATA payload and the CMD_RESPONSE
  handler pairs them by cmd_cookie before completing the se_cmd.

TMR_FORWARD (S->A)
  One per Task Management Request.  Carries session_id, cmd_cookie
  for the TMR se_cmd, ref_cookie (the command to abort for ABORT TASK),
  the TMR function code, and LUN.

TMR_RESPONSE (A->S)
  Returns the TMR response code: TMR_FUNCTION_COMPLETE or
  TMR_FUNCTION_REJECTED.

PERS_ACTION (A->S)
  Sent after each successful PR OUT executed on ACTIVE.
  Fire-and-forget -- no ACK.  Carries the PR action code, RESERVATION
  KEY, SERVICE ACTION RESERVATION KEY, reservation type, initiator
  name, device name, and fabric name.  STANDBY calls
  lio_ha_pr_apply() to update its replicated in-memory PR table.

LUN_SYNC (A->S)
  Sent by ACTIVE when the TCP link comes up, once per storage object.
  Payload: a fixed header (dev_name + aptpl_buf_len) followed by
  aptpl_buf_len bytes of APTPL-format text describing the full PR
  state for that device.  STANDBY calls lio_ha_pr_apply() for each
  registration in the text to initialise its in-memory PR table.

LUN_SYNC_DONE (A->S)
  Sent after all LUN_SYNC messages for the current sync cycle.  On
  receipt STANDBY advances ha_state from CONNECTED to SYNCED.


COMMAND FORWARDING FLOW
=======================

WRITE command:

  STANDBY                          ACTIVE
  ---------                          -------
  DATA (write data, DATA chan) -->
  CMD_FORWARD (CTL chan)        -->
                                     buffer DATA by cmd_cookie
                                     submit to backend (write_pending)
                                     backend completes
                                <--  CMD_RESPONSE (CTL chan)
  complete se_cmd with SCSI status

READ command:

  STANDBY                          ACTIVE
  ---------                          -------
  CMD_FORWARD (CTL chan)        -->
                                     submit to backend
                                     backend returns read data
                                <--  DATA (read data, DATA chan)
                                <--  CMD_RESPONSE (CTL chan)
  copy DATA to se_cmd scatter list
  complete se_cmd with SCSI status

No-data command (DMA_NONE):

  STANDBY                          ACTIVE
  ---------                          -------
  CMD_FORWARD (CTL chan)        -->
                                     submit to backend
                                     backend completes
                                <--  CMD_RESPONSE (CTL chan)
  complete se_cmd with SCSI status

PR commands (PR IN, PR OUT) go through target_execute_cmd() on
STANDBY and are forwarded using the same CMD_FORWARD/CMD_RESPONSE
path as data I/O.  A successful PR OUT on ACTIVE additionally fires
a PERS_ACTION to STANDBY so the replicated PR table stays current.


IN-FLIGHT COMMAND TRACKING
===========================

Each forwarded command is tracked on STANDBY by an ha_fwd_entry
with kref-based reference counting (initial kref = 2):

  ref 1 ("hash ref")    held while the entry is in ha_fwd_ht.
                        Dropped by the CMD_RESPONSE handler, the
                        disconnect handler, or the send-failure path.

  ref 2 ("caller ref")  held by forward_cmd() until all sends
                        complete (success or failure).  Ensures the
                        entry is not freed while forward_cmd still
                        holds a pointer to it.

target_complete_cmd() is called exactly once per entry: either by
the CMD_RESPONSE handler (success) or by the disconnect/failure
paths, which complete the se_cmd with SAM_STAT_BUSY.


HA STATE MACHINE
================

ha_state is stored in lio_ha_cfg and readable via configfs.  Both
nodes maintain the same three states, but the triggers differ:

  DISCONNECTED (0)
    TCP link is down.  On STANDBY all forwarded commands complete
    immediately with SAM_STAT_BUSY.

  CONNECTED (1)
    TCP link is up.  ACTIVE is sending LUN_SYNC messages to STANDBY.
    STANDBY stores the incoming PR state but does not yet forward
    initiator commands.

  SYNCED (2)
    PR state transfer is complete.
    ACTIVE:  advanced to SYNCED on successful send of LUN_SYNC_DONE.
    STANDBY: advanced to SYNCED on receipt of LUN_SYNC_DONE.
    Middleware reads this state to decide ALUA group states:

      Role    ha_state      local group      remote group
      ------  ------------  ---------------  ---------------
      MASTER  synced        OPTIMIZED        NONOPTIMIZED
      MASTER  connected     OPTIMIZED        TRANSITIONING
      MASTER  disconnected  OPTIMIZED        UNAVAILABLE
      BACKUP  synced        NONOPTIMIZED     OPTIMIZED
      BACKUP  connected     TRANSITIONING    OPTIMIZED
      BACKUP  disconnected  TRANSITIONING    OPTIMIZED

    "local" = this node's controller group (controller_A on Node A,
    controller_B on Node B); "remote" = the peer's group.
    LIO generates ASC 0x2A/0x06 UAs on each state write so initiators
    learn of changes without polling.

Transitions:

  DISCONNECTED -> CONNECTED       TCP connection established (both nodes)
  CONNECTED -> SYNCED             LUN_SYNC_DONE sent (ACTIVE) /
                                  LUN_SYNC_DONE received (STANDBY)
  CONNECTED/SYNCED -> DISCONNECTED  TCP link lost or closed (both nodes)


FAILOVER SEQUENCE
=================

Failover on TrueNAS is always ungraceful: the pool imports on
STANDBY while ACTIVE is unreachable.  The middleware-driven
sequence is:

  1. Middleware sets all ALUA target port groups to TRANSITIONING so
     that initiators receive the correct UA and pause I/O during the
     failover window.

  2. Middleware writes "0" to /sys/kernel/config/lio_ha/forward_active.
     lio_ha.ko processes the write as follows:

     a. lio_ha_forward_active is set to 0 immediately.  iblock and
        fileio check this flag inside configure_device(); it must be
        clear before any backend is opened.

     b. target_for_each_device() calls ha_main_failover_device() for
        every configured se_device.  Per device:

        i.  lio_ha_pr_table is scanned for entries matching this
            device and copied into a local snapshot (to avoid
            holding lio_ha_pr_lock across LIO core calls).

        ii. For each registrant, target_ha_foreach_nacl_dev() finds
            the (nacl, lun, mapped_lun) tuple in the LIO TPG
            configuration, and target_ha_pr_add_reg() installs the
            PR registration directly into LIO core.  Non-holders
            are restored first; the holder last so the reservation
            is set after all registrants exist.

        iii.target_ha_reopen_backend() calls configure_device() on
            the iblock or fileio backend to open the block device
            or file against the now-available pool.

  3. Middleware sets all ALUA target port groups to ACTIVE-OPTIMIZED.
     Initiators resume I/O against the new ACTIVE.

Writing "0" to forward_active is idempotent.  Any device whose
backend fails to open returns NOT READY; retrying the write re-runs
failover for all devices and skips those already open.


CONFIGFS ATTRIBUTES
===================

Path: /sys/kernel/config/lio_ha/

  local_addr   (rw)  IP address this node listens on for incoming HA
                     TCP connections.  Written by middleware at startup.

  peer_addr    (rw)  IP address of the peer node's HA interface.
                     ha_connect_task continuously attempts to connect
                     here.  Written by middleware at startup.

  port         (rw)  TCP port for both listen and connect.
                     Default: 999.

  ha_state     (ro)  Current state string: "disconnected", "connected",
                     or "synced".  Both nodes advance to "synced" when
                     LUN_SYNC_DONE is sent (ACTIVE) or received (STANDBY).
                     Middleware polls ha_state on both nodes and uses it
                     to set ALUA group states (see HA STATE MACHINE above).
                     "disconnected" on either node triggers an alert.

  forward_active (rw)
                     0 or 1.
                     Write "1" at STANDBY startup: sets
                     lio_ha_forward_active so backends defer open()
                     and all commands are forwarded to ACTIVE.
                     Write "0" at failover: restores replicated PR
                     state, opens all backends, clears forwarding.
                     Both writes are idempotent.


FILE MAP
========

  lio_ha_wire.h    On-wire types: lio_ha_wire_hdr, lio_ha_data_hdr,
                   lio_ha_msg_hdr, all message structs and enums, and
                   field-size constants.  No kernel dependencies;
                   safe to read in isolation.

  lio_ha.h         Internal module types: struct lio_ha_cfg, struct
                   lio_ha_pr_entry, enum lio_ha_state, and function
                   declarations shared across compilation units.

  lio_ha_main.c    Module init/exit.  Configfs subsystem registration
                   and all five attributes.  Replicated PR table
                   (lio_ha_pr_table, protected by lio_ha_pr_lock) and
                   lio_ha_pr_apply() which handles both PERS_ACTION
                   (incremental) and LUN_SYNC (bulk) updates.
                   Shadow session list (ha_main_sess_list, protected by
                   ha_main_sess_lock) for SESSION_CONNECT replay on TCP
                   link recovery (see SESSION_CONNECT above).
                   ha_main_failover_device() and the forward_active
                   write handler that drives the failover sequence.

  lio_ha_fwd.c     STANDBY-side forwarding.  Implements the four
                   lio_ha_ops callbacks (forward_cmd, forward_tmr,
                   session_create, session_destroy).  In-flight
                   command tracking with ha_fwd_entry / kref.
                   Read-data buffering (ha_fwd_data_ht, keyed by
                   cmd_cookie) for READ responses from ACTIVE.
                   VPD 0x83 response patching (lio_ha_replace_port_info)
                   so port-specific designators reflect STANDBY's port.

  lio_ha_recv.c    ACTIVE-side ha_recv fabric driver.  Single
                   system-wide TPG registered programmatically with
                   no configfs entry.  Synthetic se_session hash table
                   keyed by session_id.  CMD_FORWARD submission to LIO
                   core, WRITE-data buffering (ha_recv_data_ht), and
                   CMD_RESPONSE / DATA sending.

  lio_ha_tcp.c     TCP connection management.  Listen and connect
                   threads (both active simultaneously on each node).
                   Wire framing: lio_ha_tcp_send(), receive loop
                   (ha_rx_task) dispatching by channel and type.
                   Reconnect with exponential backoff.


DEBUGGING
=========

lio_ha_debug (module parameter, CONFIG_DEBUG_KERNEL builds only)

  Controls trace verbosity.  Set at load time:

    modprobe lio_ha lio_ha_debug=1

  or at runtime (debug builds only):

    echo 2 > /sys/module/lio_ha/parameters/lio_ha_debug

  Levels:

    0  Off (default).  No trace output beyond pr_info/pr_warn.

    1  State transitions.  SESSION_CONNECT / SESSION_DISCONNECT
       send/receive; TCP connect/disconnect events; SESSION_CONNECT
       replay count on link recovery; forward_active state changes;
       failover per-device open results.

    2  Per-command traces.  CMD_FORWARD dispatch (opcode, length,
       cookie); CMD_RESPONSE receipt; PR change events.

    3  Verbose.  LUN_SYNC per-device APTPL byte counts; PR
       registration injection details during failover.

  In non-debug kernels (CONFIG_DEBUG_KERNEL not set) the lio_ha_debug
  parameter is absent and all lio_ha_dbg() calls compile to nothing.


NAMING CONVENTIONS
==================

Functions follow a two-prefix convention:

  Public (cross-file API, declared in headers):
    lio_ha_*       e.g. lio_ha_tcp_send(), lio_ha_recv_init(),
                        lio_ha_forward_cmd(), lio_ha_pr_apply()

  Private (static, internal to each .c file):
    ha_tcp_*       lio_ha_tcp.c
    ha_fwd_*       lio_ha_fwd.c
    ha_recv_*      lio_ha_recv.c
    ha_main_*      lio_ha_main.c

Exception: the configfs show/store functions in lio_ha_main.c retain the
lio_ha_ prefix because they are named by the CONFIGFS_ATTR macro (which
takes the prefix as an argument) and changing the prefix would require
changing the user-visible configfs attribute structure names.


LIO CORE CHANGES (CONFIG_TRUENAS)
==================================

All additions in the core files are gated on #ifdef CONFIG_TRUENAS.

  include/target/target_core_ha.h
    Declares the three hooks, the target_ha_nacl_fn_t typedef, and
    all additional exports below.  Contains the core-side architecture
    overview comment.

  include/target/target_core_backend.h
    - backend_is_ready(): new optional transport op.  Returns true if
      the backend is open and ready for local execution.  Only called
      when forward_active == 0; iblock and fileio implement it by
      checking ibd_bd / fd_file for NULL.

  drivers/target/target_core_transport.c
    - lio_ha_forward_active: atomic_t.  Checked at the TOP of
      __target_execute_cmd() and target_tmr_work(), before UA, ALUA,
      and reservation checks.  Must be first so that pre-execution
      failures on STANDBY (e.g. power-on UA) never reach
      iblock_get_blocks() with a NULL ibd_bd.
    - backend_is_ready check: after the forwarding block, fails the
      command with TCM_LOGICAL_UNIT_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE if the backend
      is not yet open (deferred open failed at failover).  Prevents
      crashes in iblock execute paths and iblock_get_blocks().
    - lio_ha_register_ops() / lio_ha_unregister_ops().
    - target_ha_session_create() / target_ha_session_destroy(): thin
      wrappers around the ha_ops session hooks, exported so fabric
      drivers that do not go through target_setup_session() /
      target_remove_session() can fire the hooks directly.
    - EXPORT_SYMBOL(target_complete_tmr).

  drivers/target/target_core_iblock.c
    - iblock_configure_device(): skips the bdev_file_open() call when
      forward_active == 1 so STANDBY startup does not open the block
      device.  ibd_bd and ibd_bdev_file remain NULL until failover.
    - iblock_configure_unmap(): NULL guard for ibd_bd (deferred open);
      returns false rather than dereferencing a NULL block device.
    - iblock_get_blocks(): NULL guard for ibd_bd; returns 0 when the
      backend is not open.  Prevents a crash in target_sense_desc_format
      during sense generation for failed-reopen error state.
    - iblock_backend_is_ready(): implements the backend_is_ready op;
      returns ibd_bd != NULL.

  drivers/target/target_core_file.c
    - fd_configure_device(): skips filp_open() when forward_active == 1.
    - fd_configure_unmap(): NULL guard for fd_file (deferred open);
      returns false rather than dereferencing a NULL file pointer.
    - fd_backend_is_ready(): implements the backend_is_ready op;
      returns fd_file != NULL.

  drivers/target/target_core_pr.c
    - lio_ha_register_pr_notifier() / lio_ha_unregister_pr_notifier().
    - pr_change() call site after each successful PR OUT.
    - target_ha_pr_export(): serialise device PR state as APTPL text.
    - target_ha_pr_add_reg(): install one PR registration at failover.

  drivers/target/target_core_tpg.c
    - target_ha_lookup_tpg(): find a TPG by fabric name, target name,
      and TPG tag.
    - target_ha_foreach_nacl_dev(): iterate nacl+lun mappings for an
      initiator/device pair across all registered TPGs.
    - ha_tpg_reg_list: list of TPGs maintained by
      target_ha_register_tpg() / target_ha_unregister_tpg(), hooked
      into core_tpg_register() / core_tpg_deregister().

  drivers/target/target_core_device.c
    - target_for_each_device(): iterate all configured se_devices.
    - target_ha_reopen_backend(): open a deferred backend at failover.

  drivers/target/iscsi/iscsi_target_login.c
    - target_ha_session_create() call in iscsi_post_login_handler()
      after the session spinlock is released, in the new-session path.
      iSCSI registers sessions via __transport_register_session()
      directly (under the spinlock) rather than target_setup_session(),
      so the hook cannot be placed in the generic path.

  drivers/target/iscsi/iscsi_target.c
    - target_ha_session_destroy() call in iscsit_close_session()
      after the usage-count check confirms the session is truly being
      destroyed (not merely restarting the time2retain timer), and
      before transport_deregister_session() while se_tpg and
      se_node_acl are still valid.  iSCSI calls
      transport_deregister_session() directly rather than
      target_remove_session().
